-- title:
--   Proposition 1 (corrected: (10) implies (1); the converse fails at the final sale)
-- statement:
--   Proposition 1 asserts that, for a regular pricing policy, the restricted sub-martingale constraint (1) is equivalent to the local condition (10). This item states the half that holds and refutes the other half.
--
--   **Setting.** Fix an arrival rate $\lambda>0$, a horizon $T>0$, a lower bound $\underline\theta\ge0$ on monitoring costs, a constant $B\ge0$, an initial inventory $x_0\in\mathbb N$ and a valuation density $f$ with tail $\bar F(p)=\int_p^\infty f$. A pricing policy $\pi$ on a filtered probability space has myopic sales: the counting process $N$ (with $X=x_0-N$) has intensity $\lambda\bar F(\pi_t)$ while $X_{t-}>0$, prices are left continuous, adapted to $\mathcal G_t=\mathcal F_{t-}$, nonnegative while stock remains and $+\infty$ after sell-out. It satisfies Assumption 2 when, while $X_{t-}>0$,
--   $$
--   \pi_t=\pi^c_t+\sum_{t_n<t}\Delta_{t_n},
--   $$
--   with $\pi^c$ continuous, differentiable off the sale times, left derivative $d_t=(\partial_-/\partial t)\pi^c_t$, $\Delta$ left continuous and adapted, and $|d_t|,|\Delta_t|\le B$.
--
--   1. **Sufficiency.** If for every $s\in[0,T]$, almost surely on $\{X_{s-}>0\}$,
--   $$
--   (d_s)^-+\lambda\bar F(\pi_s)(\Delta_s)^-\le\underline\theta, \tag{10}
--   $$
--   then $\mathbb E[(\pi_s-\pi_t)^+\mid\mathcal G_s]\le\underline\theta(t-s)$ almost surely for all $0\le s\le t\le T$ (constraint (1)), where the drop is zero once stock is exhausted.
--
--   2. **The converse fails.** Take $\lambda=T=B=1$, $\underline\theta=0$, $x_0=1$ and $f(v)=e^{-v}\mathbf 1_{\{v\ge0\}}$. Post price 1 until the only sale and $+\infty$ afterwards; the sale time is exponential with rate $\bar F(1)=e^{-1}$. With $\pi^c\equiv1$, $d\equiv0$, $\Delta\equiv-1$ the policy satisfies Assumption 2 (no sale precedes $t$ while stock remains) and (1) (no price ever drops), yet (10) would require $e^{-1}\le0$.
--
--   The counterexample exploits the final sale: the prescribed infinite stockout price makes the last jump coefficient unobservable. It does not refute Theorem 2. No repaired equivalence is asserted.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The conditional expectation in (1) is encoded by testing nonnegative lintegrals on every $\mathcal G_s$-event. The second conjunct proves, rather than assumes, every admissibility property of the concrete example. See `DISPROOF.md` for the source anchors.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: the equivalence of (1) and (10) → (10) implies (1) for every regular pricing policy, together with an admissible regular robust counterexample to the converse; reason: at the last sale constraint 3 sets the price to infinity, so an unused negative finite jump cannot cause a price drop; x0=1, price 1, delta=-1, theta=0 disproves necessity (PDF 6, 15, 20–21; DISPROOF.md)
--   - reading: regular robust pricing policy satisfies (1) iff (10) → regularity and (1) are separate predicates; reason: robust already includes (1), so the printed wording is circular
--   - reading: (10) a.s. for all s → for every s in [0, T], almost surely on X_{s−} > 0; reason: the decomposition and prices are finite only while stock remains (p. 1124, constraint 3)
--   - encoding: the one-item exponential sales process and conditional constraint → a standard exponential coordinate, explicit event time, and eventwise nonnegative integrals; reason: the sale time has rate exp(-1); the drop is identically zero and the final price is actually infinity
--   - generalization: the standing valuation Assumption 1 of §2 → any nonnegative integrable valuation density supported on [0, infinity) with total mass one; reason: the local drop argument and the HJB verification use the valuation tail as a purchase probability, not monotonicity or a root of the virtual value; specializing to every source valuation law recovers the source domain without adding full support
--   - reading: the left derivative in Assumption 2 at the initial endpoint → actual left derivatives for 0<t≤T, with a bounded bookkeeping value d at t=0; condition (10) still includes s=0; reason: the source time interval starts at zero and provides no left neighbourhood there; the counterexample violates (10) also at every interior s<1 on a positive-probability event, independently of this endpoint convention
--   - encoding: the source adapted price and sales processes and their pre-time history → jointly measurable versions, natural history generated by posted prices and counts, initial price information in G_0, and simultaneous almost-sure path properties; reason: these make the source stochastic processes explicit without imposing extra density, smoothness, initial-price determinism or a revenue comparison
-- source:
--   Chen and Farias, Robust Dynamic Pricing with Strategic Customers, Mathematics of Operations Research 43(4) (2018), p. 1133, Proposition 1 and Assumption 2; pp. 1138–1139, Appendix B proof; p. 1124, constraint 3

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ChenFarias_SimplePolicy_Supplement_RegularPricing

namespace ChenFarias.SimplePolicy.Supplement

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

/-- Proposition 1, both directions as far as they hold. First conjunct: the local
condition (10) implies the restricted sub-martingale constraint (1) for every pricing
policy with Assumption 2's decomposition. Second conjunct: the converse fails as printed
at the final sale, where the prescribed infinite stockout price makes a negative finite
jump coefficient unobservable. -/
theorem proposition_1_sufficiency_and_counterexample :
    (∀ (lam theta T B : ℝ) (x0 : ℕ) (f : ℝ → ℝ),
      0 < lam → 0 ≤ theta → 0 < T → 0 ≤ B → IsDensity f →
      ∀ {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω) (P : Pricing Ω x0)
        (c delta d : ℝ → Ω → ℝ),
        IsPricing P μ lam T f → Regular P μ T B c delta d →
        LocalCondition P μ lam theta T f delta d →
        Restricted P μ theta T) ∧
    (IsDensity exampleDensity ∧
      IsPricing terminalExample (expMeasure 1) 1 1 exampleDensity ∧
      Regular terminalExample (expMeasure 1) 1 1
        (fun _ _ => 1) (fun _ _ => -1) (fun _ _ => 0) ∧
      Restricted terminalExample (expMeasure 1) 0 1 ∧
      ¬ LocalCondition terminalExample (expMeasure 1) 1 0 1 exampleDensity
        (fun _ _ => -1) (fun _ _ => 0)) := by sorry

end ChenFarias.SimplePolicy.Supplement
